```xml
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    package="com.madhub.bulkoperationtool">

    <!-- 
        The application is designed as a Bulk Operation Tool for Android Automation.
        The app enables users to automate tasks across multiple platforms like Facebook, Instagram, and TikTok.
        In a user scenario where a user wants to schedule posts or manage accounts, this tool simplifies the process without needing coding skills.
    -->
    
    <application
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.AppCompat.Light.NoActionBar">
        
        <!-- 
            MainActivity serves as the entry point for the application.
            In this scenario, when users open the app, they will be directed to the MainActivity to configure their automation settings.
        -->
        <activity android:name=".MainActivity">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
        
        <!-- 
            AutomationService handles background operations for scheduling and managing automated interactions.
            When users want to run scheduled tasks continuously in the background, this service is invoked to ensure smooth operations.
        -->
        <service android:name=".AutomationService"
                 android:exported="false" />
    </application>

    <!-- 
        Permission declarations necessary for the functionality of the bulk operation tool.
        In practical use cases, these permissions enable the app to connect to the internet and manage local storage for efficient operation.
    -->
    
    <!-- 
        The INTERNET permission is required for the application to connect to social media platforms.
        In a scenario where the user wants to post content on these platforms, this permission is essential for network communication.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    
    <!-- 
        The ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE permission allows the app to check the availability of network connections.
        In a workflow where the app attempts to post when the network is available, this permission ensures successful operation.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />
    
    <!-- 
        The WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE permission is necessary for saving any scheduled posts or logs.
        In a practical use case, users can keep track of posts and settings, ensuring they can manage their content effectively.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE" />
    
    <!-- 
        The READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE permission allows the app to access any media files from the device.
        In scenarios where users want to upload images or videos to social media, this permission facilitates the necessary file access.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE" />

</manifest>
```

### Explanation of the Code
- **Application Overview**: The manifest outlines an Android application designed for bulk operations related to social media automation. It emphasizes practical scenarios where users will benefit from scheduling and managing tasks across various platforms.
- **Activity Registration**: The `MainActivity` is the first point of interaction for users, enabling them to configure their automation preferences.
- **Service Registration**: The `AutomationService` runs background tasks, ensuring continuous operation for scheduled posts, which is essential for automation without user intervention.
- **Permission Requests**: Each permission is tied to user scenarios that reflect the application's functionality, such as needing network access for posting or accessing media for uploads, ensuring users understand why these permissions are necessary.
